 ## Background
-Memory layout optimization techniques originated from the need to overcome CPU cache hierarchy limitations and early GPU memory controller constraints. Hardware-specific optimizations, such as miniblock organization and bank skewing, were developed to exploit the burst capabilities of SDRAM in dedicated rendering hardware [S1, unofficial]. In the context of GPU-based isosurface extraction, canonical term optimization reduces memory bandwidth pressure by restructuring how field terms are stored and accessed [S2, unofficial]. Sparse allocation techniques, as demonstrated in the Avalanche grid presentation by Jerry Tessendorf at Rhythm & Hues Studios, show that avoiding allocation of empty space is highly effective for large, sparse volumes [S4, unofficial]. Out-of-core techniques like [Memory-Insensitive Rendering](2774-memory-insensitive-rendering.md) were introduced to handle datasets exceeding available RAM by bypassing memory bottlenecks via external sorting.
